"# The History of the Printing Press\n\n*A research report based on English Wikipedia*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe printing press is a device for applying pressure to an inked surface resting upon a print medium (typically paper or cloth), enabling the mass production of printed text. While the underlying concepts of printing originated in East Asia centuries earlier, the movable-type printing press developed by **Johannes Gutenberg** in Mainz, Germany (c. 1440–1450) is regarded as one of the most influential inventions in human history, triggering the \"Printing Revolution\" that reshaped European — and eventually global — society [1][2].\n\n---\n\n## 2. Before Gutenberg: East Asian Precedents\n\n### 2.1 Woodblock Printing\n\nPrinting in East Asia predates Gutenberg's press by centuries:\n\n- **China:** Woodblock printing developed during the Tang dynasty (7th century CE), initially used for textiles and later for text. The **Diamond Sutra (868 CE)**, discovered at Dunhuang, is the world's oldest surviving dated printed book [1][3].\n- **Japan:** The *Hyakumantō Darani* (c. 764–770) was among the earliest mass-printed texts [3].\n- **Korea:** The **Tripitaka Koreana** (1236–1251) comprises over 81,000 woodblocks at Haeinsa Temple — one of the most complete Buddhist canons ever produced [4].\n\n### 2.2 Movable Type in China\n\nThe world's **first movable type system** was invented by **Bi Sheng** (c. 970–1051), a commoner artisan of the Northern Song dynasty, around **1041–1048 CE**. He used individual characters carved from baked clay/porcelain, set into an iron frame and secured with pine resin and wax. His method is documented in Shen Kuo's *Dream Pool Essays* (1088). Later Chinese refinements included **Wang Zhen's** wooden movable type and rotating typecase (c. 1297) and **Hua Sui's** bronze type (c. 1490) [3][5].\n\n### 2.3 Metal Movable Type in Korea\n\nKorea developed the most sustained pre-Gutenberg metal-type program:\n\n- The *Goryeosa* records the casting of bronze type in **1234** to print the *Sangjeong Gogeum Yemun*, though no copies survive [6].\n- The ***Jikji*** (1377), printed with movable metal type at Heungdeok Temple in Cheongju, is the **oldest extant book printed with metal movable type** — predating the Gutenberg Bible by 78 years. It is held at the Bibliothèque nationale de France and was inscribed on UNESCO's Memory of the World Register in 2001 [7].\n- In 1403, King Taejong of Joseon established a national foundry (Jujaso) for government type casting, and under **King Sejong the Great**, refined typefaces were cast and **Hangul** (1443) — an alphabet exceptionally suited to movable type — was created [6].\n\nWhether Gutenberg's invention was influenced by East Asian printing via the Silk Road or was wholly independent remains debated by scholars [3][8]. Notably, these East Asian systems did not achieve mass production, partly because Chinese writing's thousands of logographic characters made type-based printing less economical than alphabetic scripts [3].\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gutenberg's Invention (c. 1440–1455)\n\n### 3.1 The Inventor\n\n**Johannes Gutenberg** (c. 1400–1468), a goldsmith from Mainz, began experiments in **Strasbourg around 1440** and perfected his system in Mainz by about 1450 [2][9]. His genius lay not in inventing printing itself, but in **combining existing technologies into a complete, practical, scalable printing system** [2][8].\n\n### 3.2 Key Innovations\n\n1. **Metal movable type and the hand mould** — An adjustable mould enabled rapid, identical casting of individual type pieces in a durable **lead–tin–antimony alloy** (chosen for its low melting point, clean mold-filling, and durability). The punch–matrix–casting workflow remained standard for roughly 500 years [2][8].\n2. **Oil-based ink** — Water-based inks used for woodblocks did not adhere to metal, so Gutenberg formulated a sticky ink of boiled **linseed oil** mixed with soot/lampblack that bonded to metal type and transferred cleanly to paper or vellum [2][10].\n3. **Adaptation of the screw press** — He adapted the screw press used for wine, olives, and papermaking to apply firm, **even pressure across the whole page**, superior to the rubbing methods of woodblock printing. A movable bed/carriage allowed quick page changes and high throughput [2][8].\n\n### 3.3 The Gutenberg Bible\n\nFinanced by loans from **Johann Fust** (800 guilders, later 1,600 more), Gutenberg produced the **42-line Gutenberg Bible (c. 1454–1455)** — the first major book printed with movable metal type in Europe [2][9][11]:\n\n- Latin Vulgate text, 42 lines per page in two columns, two volumes totaling ~1,286 pages\n- Edition of roughly **180 copies** (~135–150 on paper, 30–45 on vellum)\n- **Textura (blackletter)** typeface with ~290 distinct sorts, designed to mimic hand-copied manuscripts\n- Rubrication and decorated initials added by hand; paper imported from Italian mills\n- Copies sold for **30 florins** — about three years' wages for a clerk\n- **~49 copies survive today** (about 21 complete); notable copies are held by the Library of Congress, the British Library, and the Morgan Library & Museum [11]\n\nIn a **1455 lawsuit**, Fust won control of most of Gutenberg's equipment and continued printing with **Peter Schöffer** [2][9].\n\n### 3.4 Productivity Leap\n\nA single Gutenberg-style press could produce roughly **3,600 pages per workday**, compared with only a few pages by a hand copyist — a productivity gain of several orders of magnitude that made mass production of text feasible for the first time [1][12].\n\n---\n\n## 4. Spread Across Europe (1450–1600)\n\n### 4.1 Diffusion\n\nThe **1462 Sack of Mainz** dispersed German printers, accelerating the technology's spread [1]. Carried largely by German craftsmen, printing reached [1][13]:\n\n| Region | First Press |\n|---|---|\n| Italy | Subiaco, 1465; Venice, 1469 |\n| France | Paris, 1470 |\n| Spain | c. 1472–74 |\n| England | Westminster (William Caxton), 1476 |\n\nBy **1500**, presses operated in **more than 250 European towns and cities**, producing an estimated **20 million volumes**. Books printed before 1501 are called **incunabula** — roughly 27,000–30,000 known distinct editions [1][14]. The 16th century saw an estimated **150–200 million copies** printed, with presses in nearly every major European city by 1600 [1][12].\n\n**Venice** became Europe's leading printing center. **Aldus Manutius** (Aldine Press, founded c. 1494) pioneered **italic type** (1501) and pocket-sized, affordable editions of the classics [1][12].\n\n### 4.2 The Printing Revolution and Its Cultural Impact\n\nHistorians — most influentially **Elizabeth Eisenstein** — frame print as the engine of transformations across early modern Europe [1][12]:\n\n- **The Reformation:** Luther's **95 Theses (1517)** spread throughout Germany within about two weeks and across Europe within two months. Luther was the era's most published author — roughly **one-fifth to one-third of all German books printed between 1518 and 1525** were his. Cheap pamphlets (*Flugschriften*) carried Reformation arguments to mass audiences, and Luther's **German Bible** (New Testament 1522; complete 1534) democratized access to scripture. Historians such as Andrew Pettegree argue the Reformation was the first mass-media movement, unlikely to have succeeded without print [15].\n- **The Renaissance:** Print accelerated the dissemination of recovered classical texts, enabled textual criticism and philology (e.g., Erasmus's 1516 Greek New Testament), and created a trans-European \"republic of letters\" [12][16].\n- **The Scientific Revolution:** Exact reproduction of diagrams, maps, tables, and data underpinned the work of Copernicus, Vesalius, and others [12].\n- **Language and literacy:** Printing standardized vernacular languages, strengthened them alongside Latin, and expanded literacy and the professions of authorship and publishing [12].\n\n### 4.3 Censorship and Early News\n\nAuthorities responded to print's power with censorship — most notably the Catholic Church's **Index Librorum Prohibitorum (1559)** and England's Licensing Acts [1][15]. The first printed **weekly newspaper** appeared in Strasbourg in **1605** — Johann Carolus's *Relation* [1].\n\n---\n\n## 5. Industrial-Era and Modern Advances\n\nGutenberg's wooden screw press remained essentially unchanged for **~350 years** before mechanization transformed printing [1]:\n\n| Year | Innovation | Inventor | Significance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1800 | All-iron hand press | Lord Stanhope | Larger platen, less effort required [1] |\n| 1814 | **Steam-powered cylinder press** | Friedrich Koenig (with Andreas Bauer) | *The Times* of London printed by steam on 29 November 1814 — ~1,100 sheets/hour vs. ~250 by hand; Koenig & Bauer (founded 1817) remains the world's oldest operating press manufacturer [17] |\n| 1843–47 | **Rotary press** | Richard March Hoe | Type curved on a rotating cylinder; ~8,000–10,000 impressions/hour (\"Hoe Lightning\" presses) [18] |\n| 1863–65 | **Web-fed rotary press** | William Bullock | Continuous paper rolls, automatic feeding, printing both sides simultaneously — backbone of mass-circulation newspapers [18] |\n| 1884–86 | **Linotype** | Ottmar Mergenthaler | Keyboard-operated machine casting entire lines of type as single metal slugs; first installed at the *New York Tribune* (1886); Thomas Edison reportedly called it \"the eighth wonder of the world\" [19] |\n| 1796–98 | **Lithography** | Alois Senefelder | Chemical printing foundation for later offset methods [20] |\n| 1904 | **Offset lithography** | Ira Washington Rubel | Rubber-blanket transfer produced sharper impressions; became the **dominant commercial printing method of the 20th century** [20] |\n\nThe 20th century brought phototypesetting, **laser printing** (Xerox, 1970s), and **desktop publishing** (1980s) — culminating in what has been called the \"digital printing revolution\" [1][18].\n\n---\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe history of the printing press spans nearly two millennia, from Chinese woodblocks and Bi Sheng's ceramic movable type (c. 1040) to Korea's metal-type *Jikji* (1377), through Gutenberg's integrated system (c. 1450), and onward through steam, rotary, Linotype, and offset technologies to today's digital publishing. Gutenberg's decisive contribution was synthesizing movable metal type, the hand mould, oil-based ink, and the screw press into a scalable system that collapsed the cost of reproducing text. In doing so, it enabled the Reformation, accelerated the Renaissance and Scientific Revolution, standardized vernacular languages, and laid the foundation for modern mass communication and universal literacy [1][2][12].\n\n---\n\n## Sources (English Wikipedia articles)\n\n1. *Printing press*\n2. *Johannes Gutenberg*\n3. *History of printing in East Asia* / *Movable type*\n4. *Tripitaka Koreana*\n5. *Bi Sheng* / *Wang Zhen (inventor)*\n6. *History of printing in Korea* / *Hangul*\n7. *Jikji*\n8. *Printing press* / *Johannes Gutenberg* (influence debate)\n9. *Johannes Gutenberg*\n10. *Ink*\n11. *Gutenberg Bible*\n12. *Printing revolution* / *Printing press*\n13. *Global spread of the printing press*\n14. *Incunable*\n15. *Martin Luther* / *Index Librorum Prohibitorum* / *Reformation*\n16. *Renaissance humanism*\n17. *Friedrich Koenig* / *Koenig & Bauer*\n18. *Rotary printing press* / *Richard March Hoe* / *William Bullock*\n19. *Linotype* / *Ottmar Mergenthaler*\n20. *Offset printing* / *Lithography*"